            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/include/utils/collection.hpp",
                    "line": 101,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't know why we are deleting duplicate leaves, but I don't think that is correct.\n\nTwo different dbus objects are different entities.  We need to create two different REST end points for them, not attempt to merge them into a single (now undefined) entity.\n\nI gave an example in the test case of \"bios1/xyz123\" and \"bios2/xyz123\".  Do you really want those to show up as a single firmware object?  How do you target the specific one you want to update if you're only updating a single BIOS (host)?  How do we associate them to the hardware they're running on (because each one is running on a different hardware)?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/include/utils/collection.hpp",
                    "line": 101,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Prabha Veerubhotla",
                        "email": "vvlprabha@gmail.com",
                        "username": "Prabha-Veerubhotla"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "test/redfish-core/include/utils/collection_test.cpp",
                    "line": 170,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "These examples are somewhat problematic and hide the problem that we are seeing.  You'd be better showing `bios1/xyz789` and `bios2/xyz789` because that's the behavior we're actually running into.\n\nThose are two different software entities on two different pieces of hardware.  They just happen to have the same leaf node."
                },
                {
